<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free People's State of Württemberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1945 CE · capital Stuttgart</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Democratic successor of the Kingdom of Württemberg, a well-governed Weimar state that was split by the occupying powers in 1945.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In November 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-wurttemberg">King William II</a> abdicated, and a provisional government of Social Democrats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">Independents</a> declared a republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. A constitution adopted in 1919 created the Free People's State of Württemberg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Württemberg had a balanced economy of small farms and skilled industry, with companies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bosch-gmbh">Bosch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimler-motoren-gesellschaft">Daimler</a>. After governments led by Social Democrats and liberals, the state was led by conservatives and from 1928 by the Centre's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugen-bolz">Eugen Bolz</a>, who was later executed for his part in the resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Hitler</a>. After 1933 the Nazi Reich governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-murr">Wilhelm Murr</a> ruled the state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1945 the state was divided by the occupying powers: the northern part, in the American zone, joined northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> as Württemberg-Baden, and the southern part, under the French, became Württemberg-Hohenzollern. They merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> in 1952.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Free People's State of Württemberg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_K%C3%B6nigreich_W%C3%BCrttemberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stuttgart</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eugen Bolz, Wilhelm Murr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Swabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freier Volksstaat Württemberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg" aria-label="Free People's State of Württemberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 474 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free People's State of Württemberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 730L5212 730M5212 730L5300 622M5212 730L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5212 730h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stuttgart. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
